1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,800
 So I was a student at MIT, and I was scheduled on the wonderful scholarship program supported

2
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:11,680
 by MIT Lincoln Labs to get a master's degree. And it was a very rigid schedule. You're going

3
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:17,060
 to be done in two years, get a job at Lincoln Lab, and do research. And so I was on the

4
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:22,160
 program, and I was one of the few people who actually finished on time. And as I was finishing,

5
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:29,080
 I scheduled to have my first child the summer of 1958, when we were going to be done. In

6
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:35,280
 that summer, my professor at the server mechanisms lab at MIT said, "You have to get a PhD."

7
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:39,760
 I said, "I don't want to get a PhD. I've done this program. I'm scheduled. My child

8
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:44,240
 has just been born. So you've got to do it." So I kept twisting my arm, and finally I said,

9
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:47,920
 "Okay, I'll do it. But I'm going to do it. I want to work on something that's really

10
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:53,280
 significant and not pittle around for the next three or four years." So I decided I'm

11
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:58,640
 going to work for the best professor I know at MIT, and that was Claude Shannon, the infamous,

12
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:03,720
 wonderful, magnificent person, Claude Shannon. So I called him up, and he brought me to his

13
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:07,920
 house, and we chatted a bit. Decided, yes, we're going to work together. The first thing

14
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:12,240
 I started working on, actually, was a chess playing program. Just working with a man was

15
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:19,440
 a delight. He was a great engineer, a great mathematician, smart as heck, could solve

16
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:24,720
 a problem like that. I looked around at my classmates, most of whom were working for

17
00:01:24,720 --> 00:01:28,880
 him, and most of them were working on the kind of work he had developed, namely the

18
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:35,760
 field of information theory and coding theory. I looked at them, and I had taken those courses,

19
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:39,780
 and I said, "You know, the work that they're doing is the work that he left behind. Those

20
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:44,480
 problems that are left over are hard and probably not of great significance, little bits and

21
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:49,480
 pieces left." I said, "That's not what I signed up for. I wanted something to be fun,

22
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:55,520
 exciting, challenging, and have impact." Meanwhile, being at MIT and Lincoln Laboratory,

23
00:01:55,520 --> 00:02:00,600
 I was surrounded by computers, and I recognized, you know, one day these computers are going

24
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:04,920
 to have to talk to each other, because this is way early. Nobody was thinking about that

25
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:08,400
 at the time, but I said, "If they're going to talk to each other, what technology would

26
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:15,040
 support their communication and their interaction?" And the answer is, there was nothing available.

27
00:02:15,040 --> 00:02:20,240
 So I had an approach. I figured the way to provide the communications would be to provide

28
00:02:20,240 --> 00:02:26,280
 the ability for shared communication links, because I knew that computers, when they talk,

29
00:02:26,280 --> 00:02:30,680
 they don't talk the way I am now continuously. They go blast, and they're quiet for a while.

30
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:35,840
 Little while later, they suddenly come up and blast again. And you can't afford to dedicate

31
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:41,280
 a communications connection for something which is almost never talking. But when it

32
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:46,240
 wants to talk, it wants immediate access. So we had to not use the telephone network,

33
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:50,640
 which is designed for continuous talking, the circuit switch network, but something

34
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:57,360
 else. And I had an approach using some mathematical thinking, namely queuing theory. There was

35
00:02:57,360 --> 00:03:02,680
 a model I could develop, and it just made sense to do that which we had done in time

36
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:07,920
 sharing. In time sharing, you have a big computer, and a lot of people share it. When you're

37
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:13,580
 using it, I'm not. When you're not, I am. So we sort of interlace each other. Why not

38
00:03:13,580 --> 00:03:19,080
 use communications the same way? We're going to set up communications capability, let everybody

39
00:03:19,080 --> 00:03:23,580
 jump in and share it, and they only get to use it when they have something to send. This

40
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:28,360
 is a new technology. So there's an approach. I have a mathematical tool to do it. It's

41
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:32,800
 clearly important. It will have impact. And nobody's looked at this yet, so there's a

42
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:38,440
 lot of low-hanging fruit around. It's not that hard. It's not that hard. So it's perfect

43
00:03:38,440 --> 00:03:44,420
 for me. So I started working on that problem. Now, this was years before anybody needed

44
00:03:44,420 --> 00:03:49,360
 this technology. In time sharing, for example, which is the underlying technology that I

45
00:03:49,360 --> 00:03:54,680
 adapted to communications, in time sharing, you want to let the little jobs go before

46
00:03:54,680 --> 00:03:59,520
 the big jobs. Why should a little guy wait a long time for a big job? He's just going

47
00:03:59,520 --> 00:04:04,080
 to get in and get out. So when jobs come in, you ask them, "How long are you?" And they're

48
00:04:04,080 --> 00:04:07,940
 all going to say, "I'm tiny." So you say, "Okay, you're tiny. I'll give you a little

49
00:04:07,940 --> 00:04:12,320
 bit of time. And if you're tiny, you'll be out of there. No? I'll send you to the back

50
00:04:12,320 --> 00:04:18,080
 of the queue and you get another tiny shot." The notion of round robin. So you get a little

51
00:04:18,080 --> 00:04:22,720
 bit at a time. We're going to break you into little chunks. You get a chunk at a time.

52
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:27,600
 I said, "That's a great idea for sharing communications. We'll give everybody a little

53
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:32,080
 bit of communications time. The little ones will filter out. The short jobs will filter

54
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:38,560
 out." When I say job, I mean message. A short message will filter out. And the long ones

55
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:42,200
 will take a little longer. And they don't mind being interrupted by the little guys.

56
00:04:42,200 --> 00:04:47,380
 The important thing in this technology was not to make the very short messages wait behind

57
00:04:47,380 --> 00:04:53,640
 very long messages. This automatic round robin, which is now called packet switching, you

58
00:04:53,640 --> 00:04:58,600
 chop things into fixed lengths. You give them a little bit. If that's not enough, give them

59
00:04:58,600 --> 00:05:04,240
 another piece and they go flying through the network on their own. So the idea of packetizing

60
00:05:04,240 --> 00:05:08,480
 was important. The idea of distributed control, the idea of large shared systems in which

61
00:05:08,480 --> 00:05:13,700
 you get some terrific design benefits came along. And so this got published. McGraw-Hill

62
00:05:13,700 --> 00:05:16,400
 book. Nobody cared.

63
00:05:16,400 --> 00:05:18,320
 It got published early?

64
00:05:18,320 --> 00:05:24,480
 Yeah. My dissertation ended in December '62. I graduated in June '63. This book was published

65
00:05:24,480 --> 00:05:25,480
 in 1964.

66
00:05:25,480 --> 00:05:26,480
 And nobody cared?

67
00:05:26,480 --> 00:05:30,880
 Nobody cared. In fact, I went to AT&T, the biggest network at the time, and explained

68
00:05:30,880 --> 00:05:35,320
 to them, "You guys ought to give us good data communications." And their answer was, "What

69
00:05:35,320 --> 00:05:39,080
 are you talking about? The United States is a copper mine. It's full of telephone wires.

70
00:05:39,080 --> 00:05:44,800
 Use that." And I said, "No, no. You don't understand. It takes you 35 seconds to set

71
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:49,400
 up a call. You charge me a minimum of three minutes, and I want to send 100 milliseconds

72
00:05:49,400 --> 00:05:55,760
 of data." And their answer was, "Little boy, go away." So little boy went away and with

73
00:05:55,760 --> 00:06:00,300
 others developed this technology which ate their lunch. But nobody cared. And they said

74
00:06:00,300 --> 00:06:05,240
 it wouldn't work. And even if it did work, they want nothing to do with it. So that was

75
00:06:05,240 --> 00:06:10,580
 the environment we faced. It wasn't until years later when the government decided they

76
00:06:10,580 --> 00:06:15,240
 needed a network that suddenly I saw a way in which I could implement the technology

77
00:06:15,240 --> 00:06:16,680
 I had.

78
00:06:16,680 --> 00:06:21,920
 But getting back to what I said earlier, I set up this mathematical model. It was analytically

79
00:06:21,920 --> 00:06:26,960
 intractable and still is, by the way. I had two choices. One is give up and find something

80
00:06:26,960 --> 00:06:33,920
 else. Or two, make an assumption which allows me to move forward. So I introduced a mathematical

81
00:06:33,920 --> 00:06:39,280
 assumption which cracked the problem wide open. From that point, I could just sail through

82
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:45,840
 the solution, get the performance behavior, get the design principles, going forward.

83
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:48,200
 But then the question is, was that assumption any good?

84
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:50,120
 What was the assumption?

85
00:06:50,120 --> 00:06:56,720
 The assumption was what I call the independence assumption. And it's absolutely a false assumption.

86
00:06:56,720 --> 00:07:02,240
 It says when a message travels through the network, it changes its length independently

87
00:07:02,240 --> 00:07:05,960
 every time it hits a new node on its hop through the network.

88
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:10,640
 Mathematically, that creates an independence which allows you to proceed with the analysis.

89
00:07:10,640 --> 00:07:12,760
 But it's clearly not true.

90
00:07:12,760 --> 00:07:18,240
 So what I had to do was to simulate a network with and without the assumption. I simulated

91
00:07:18,240 --> 00:07:24,320
 many networks on a machine at Lincoln Laboratory on the TX2 transistorized computer there.

92
00:07:24,320 --> 00:07:30,280
 And I spent four months writing the simulation program without debugging a single line of

93
00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:38,920
 it at a 2,500 line assembly language simulation. And I knew if I didn't get that simulation

94
00:07:38,920 --> 00:07:44,880
 right, I would get no dissertation. Ran it, tested with and without the assumption, and

95
00:07:44,880 --> 00:07:50,480
 the results were amazingly close. So I had my solution. I could prove it would work.

96
00:07:50,480 --> 00:07:54,240
 The package wouldn't fall on the floor. I could tell when things would work well and

97
00:07:54,240 --> 00:07:58,440
 when they wouldn't. Got published. Again, still nobody cared about it.

98
00:07:58,440 --> 00:08:02,080
 As I said, Lincoln Laboratory sent me for my master's dissertation. They also sent me

99
00:08:02,080 --> 00:08:09,440
 for my PhD. So they supported me for years. Wonderful economic, financial, research environment

100
00:08:09,440 --> 00:08:14,360
 support. And I felt an obligation to work for them. And I was prepared to take a job

101
00:08:14,360 --> 00:08:21,160
 at Lincoln Laboratory, which is a great institution. And so when I went to work there, the first

102
00:08:21,160 --> 00:08:25,660
 thing they said is, "Look, Glenn, why don't you look outside before you can commit to

103
00:08:25,660 --> 00:08:30,080
 work here? Make sure there's nothing out there that you really would like better."

104
00:08:30,080 --> 00:08:37,420
 This was a magnificent step on their part. So I took a trip to the West Coast. I went

105
00:08:37,420 --> 00:08:44,140
 to some of the aerospace companies. I was not interested in university position at all.

106
00:08:44,140 --> 00:08:47,560
 But it turns out when I was going up to San Francisco to look at some of the high-tech

107
00:08:47,560 --> 00:08:55,520
 companies up there, a friend of mine suggested I interview with Berkeley. So I did. I interviewed.

108
00:08:55,520 --> 00:09:02,320
 I came back. They lost my case. They changed the chairman. I never heard from them. But

109
00:09:02,320 --> 00:09:06,200
 on sabbatical, one of the professors that interviewed me came to MIT. Well, I was just

110
00:09:06,200 --> 00:09:11,140
 finishing my PhD then. And he sees me in the hall and says, "Cleanrock, how are you?"

111
00:09:11,140 --> 00:09:16,840
 And he thinks that I'm looking for an academic position. So he contacts one of his friends

112
00:09:16,840 --> 00:09:23,080
 here at UCLA, who then invites me out here, offers me a job. And now I've got a dilemma.

113
00:09:23,080 --> 00:09:27,280
 Do I want to teach? Do I want to cross the United States? It's almost like on a wagon

114
00:09:27,280 --> 00:09:33,400
 train 3,000 miles away from the East Coast, where the world is, family is, for a job paying

115
00:09:33,400 --> 00:09:40,240
 half that I could earn back at MIT Lincoln Labs and try something I've never tried before.

116
00:09:40,240 --> 00:09:44,640
 So I went to the books at Lincoln Lab and I said, "Look, I've got this offer. It looks

117
00:09:44,640 --> 00:09:50,640
 attractive. It's a new challenge. What should I do?" And there was a wonderful answer they

118
00:09:50,640 --> 00:09:57,760
 gave. They said, "Lenn, try it. If you don't like it, come back." Wow is right. Well, I

119
00:09:57,760 --> 00:10:02,120
 came here in August of '63, 50 years ago, and I'm still here.

